<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>PathKeeper | Multi-Outcome Experimentation Framework</title>
  <style>
    * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
    
    body {
      font-family: 'Segoe UI', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, Roboto, sans-serif;
      background: #06130e;
      color: #e8f3ed;
      line-height: 1.5;
      padding: 16px;
    }

    .container { 
      width: 100%; 
      max-width: 900px; 
      margin: 0 auto; 
    }
    
    /* Header */
    header {
      background: #0f2d22;
      border: 1px solid #1f4738;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 22px 24px;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    .badge-tag {
      color: #72ecb7;
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 1px;
    }

    h1 { 
      font-size: 1.6rem; 
      color: #ffffff; 
      font-weight: 800; 
      margin-top: 4px;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    header p {
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      color: #b0c9bc;
    }

    /* Core Sections */
    .card {
      background: #0d261e;
      border: 1px solid #1f4738;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 20px;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    .card h2 { 
      font-size: 1.15rem; 
      color: #dcb386; 
      margin-bottom: 10px; 
      border-bottom: 1px solid #1f4738;
      padding-bottom: 6px;
    }

    .card h3 {
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      color: #72ecb7;
      margin: 14px 0 6px 0;
    }

    p, li {
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      color: #c5dcd0;
    }

    ul, ol {
      margin-left: 18px;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    li {
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    /* Highlight Callouts */
    .callout {
      background: #071712;
      border-left: 3px solid #dcb386;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
      margin: 12px 0;
    }

    .callout p {
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      color: #e8f3ed;
    }

    /* Framework Table */
    .data-table {
      width: 100%;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      margin: 12px 0;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }

    .data-table th, .data-table td {
      border: 1px solid #183b2e;
      padding: 8px 10px;
      text-align: left;
    }

    .data-table th {
      background: #071712;
      color: #72ecb7;
      font-weight: 700;
    }

    .data-table td {
      background: #0a1f18;
    }

    /* Grid layouts */
    .grid-2 {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 12px;
      margin-top: 10px;
    }

    .grid-3 {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
      gap: 12px;
      margin-top: 10px;
    }

    .sub-card {
      background: #071712;
      border: 1px solid #183b2e;
      padding: 12px;
      border-radius: 6px;
    }

    .sub-card h4 {
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      color: #dcb386;
      margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 650px) {
      .grid-2, .grid-3 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <div class="container">
    
    <!-- HEADER -->
    <header>
      <span class="badge-tag">PathKeeper Architecture</span>
      <h1>Multi-Outcome Experimentation Framework</h1>
      <p>How to design, track, and run low-risk operational experiments in your daily workflow without relying on digital web tools.</p>
    </header>

    <!-- SECTION 1: THE FOUNDATION -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>01. Why Off-Site Experimentation Matters</h2>
      <p>True strategic resilience isn't built inside web applications—it is built in your physical routine, personal decision journals, and operational workflows. Digital interactive tools can simulate ideas, but real-world execution happens offline.</p>
      
      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Core Principle:</strong> An experiment is any small, controlled test designed to gather real feedback before committing full energy, time, or capital to a single path.</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Key Advantages of Offline Testing:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Frictionless Setup:</strong> No login credentials, app crashes, or digital fatigue.</li>
        <li><strong>High Intentionality:</strong> Writing by hand or tracking on physical scorecards increases focus and retention.</li>
        <li><strong>Immediate Reality Check:</strong> Offline actions force interaction with actual daily variables rather than hypothetical digital models.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 2: 4 OFFLINE EXPERIMENT METHODS -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>02. Methods for Offline Experimentation</h2>
      <p>You can execute multi-outcome strategies immediately using four primary low-tech structures:</p>

      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="sub-card">
          <h4>1. Notebook & Decision Log</h4>
          <p>Dedicate a section of a notebook to record pre-decision baselines. Document your primary expectation, two backup routes, and a trigger condition for switching tracks.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sub-card">
          <h4>2. Physical Wall Board</h4>
          <p>Use index cards or post-it notes on a board to map your core project into three lanes: <em>Upside Focus</em>, <em>Baseline Maintenance</em>, and <em>Risk Guardrails</em>.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sub-card">
          <h4>3. Offline Spreadsheets</h4>
          <p>Maintain a simple local CSV or Excel ledger to track weekly metrics, effort allocation percentages, and early warning indicators for your active pursuits.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sub-card">
          <h4>4. Time-Boxed Pilot Runs</h4>
          <p>Test a new process or offering for a strict 7-day or 14-day window in the physical world before committing to a permanent schedule or expense.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 3: STEP-BY-STEP EXECUTION -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>03. Step-by-Step Blueprint for an Offline Experiment</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Define the Target Initiative:</strong> Select a single priority (e.g., launching a new project, refining a workflow, or shifting scheduling models).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Map the 4 Possible Outcomes:</strong>
          <ul>
            <li><em>Best Case:</em> Demand or efficiency exceeds expectations.</li>
            <li><em>Expected Case:</em> Stable, steady progression along the main plan.</li>
            <li><em>Downside Strain:</em> Time or resource costs exceed the estimate.</li>
            <li><em>External Shift:</em> Unforeseen changes alter the landscape.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Set Trigger Points:</strong> Write down specific numbers or dates that tell you when to shift from Outcome A to Outcome B.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run a 10-Day Field Test:</strong> Execute the plan locally. Log daily observations in a physical notebook at the end of each shift or workday.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review & Adjust:</strong> Compare actual results against your initial 4 outcome notes. Refine your system based on real evidence.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- NEW SECTION 4: DIAGNOSTIC & STRESS TESTING -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>04. Pre-Mortem Stress Testing Protocol</h2>
      <p>Before launching any offline test, run your plan through a pre-mortem audit to spot hidden weaknesses before they cost real-world time or resources.</p>
      
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="sub-card">
          <h4>1. Assume Absolute Failure</h4>
          <p>Fast-forward 30 days. Assume the experiment failed completely. List the top 3 physical or logistical breakdowns that caused it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sub-card">
          <h4>2. Identify Friction Points</h4>
          <p>Where does execution rely on perfect energy, perfect timing, or unverified help? Remove single points of failure early.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sub-card">
          <h4>3. Build Immediate Safeguards</h4>
          <p>Adjust your starting rules to eliminate those vulnerabilities before day one of the field test.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- NEW SECTION 5: RESOURCE ALLOCATION -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>05. Offline Resource & Energy Guardrails</h2>
      <p>Multi-outcome execution requires protecting your primary baseline while running tests on the side. Use this 70/20/10 split to protect daily stability:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>70% Baseline Energy:</strong> Committed to essential, income-generating, or non-negotiable daily operations.</li>
        <li><strong>20% Managed Testing:</strong> Dedicated strictly to running active offline experiments and monitoring triggers.</li>
        <li><strong>10% Contingency Buffer:</strong> Left completely uncommitted to absorb unexpected delays, personal fatigue, or physical disruptions.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 6: FRAMEWORK MATRIX -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>06. Off-Site Experimentation Template</h2>
      <p>Recreate this layout in your journal, on a whiteboard, or in a local document to structure your planning:</p>

      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Outcome Tier</th>
            <th>Operational Focus</th>
            <th>Trigger Condition</th>
            <th>Offline Action Step</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tier 1: Growth</strong></td>
            <td>Full expansion & aggressive execution</td>
            <td>80%+ target milestone achieved early</td>
            <td>Reinvest excess capacity into scale</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tier 2: Baseline</strong></td>
            <td>Standard daily routine & steady output</td>
            <td>Metrics remain within expected range</td>
            <td>Maintain current resource split</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tier 3: Defensive</strong></td>
            <td>Protect core stability & conserve energy</td>
            <td>Time or resource strain exceeds +20%</td>
            <td>Cut secondary tasks; preserve core output</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Tier 4: Pivot</strong></td>
            <td>Activate backup route or contingency</td>
            <td>External blocker halts primary path</td>
            <td>Switch immediately to pre-planned Route B</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- NEW SECTION 7: AUDIT & RETROSPECTIVE -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>07. Weekly Offline Audit & Retrospective</h2>
      <p>At the end of every week, answer these four reflection questions in your physical log to keep your strategic capability sharp:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Which of the 4 outcome scenarios did reality most closely resemble this week?</li>
        <li>Did any unexpected variables appear that were missing from your initial setup?</li>
        <li>Were your trigger points clear enough to prompt action without hesitation?</li>
        <li>What single adjustment will make next week’s execution more resilient?</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION 8: SUMMARY -->
    <div class="card">
      <h2>08. Summary</h2>
      <p>By keeping experimentation offline, you build practical problem-solving habits that work anywhere—regardless of technology access, software changes, or digital distractions. Strategy is measured by real-world stability, clarity, and execution.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

</body>
</html>
